  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">1. GRANT SET, non-2xx (including 429): the grant query's failure is NOT cached — after the call, no entry exists at the grant key, and the next call re-queries. The faulted request itself composes no grant-derived access (unchanged fail-closed).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">2. GRANT SET, exception and timeout: same as 1. A TaskCanceledException with the caller's token NOT cancelled is a fault (not cached, not propagated as cancellation).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">3. GRANT SET, caller cancellation: with the caller's token cancelled, OperationCanceledException propagates out of GetGrantSetAsync and nothing is cached.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">4. GRANT SET, partial: an org-grant sub-query fault, or task 109's junction Unreadable outcome, returns the successfully-read direct grants for this request and caches NOTHING.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">5. GRANT SET, legitimate empty (negative over-correction case): a successful query returning zero rows IS cached, and the next call is a cache hit.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">6. IDENTITY: a fault on each sub-path — systemuser row, teammembership, contact cross-ref, account, an organization resolver — produces an identity that is NOT cached (five tests, one per path); the next ResolveAsync re-reads.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">7. IDENTITY, legitimate absence: a successful resolution with no teams, no primary contact and no organizations IS cached.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">8. MEMBERSHIP: on the systemuser path, a response built on a faulted identity is NOT cached; one built on a clean identity with zero matches IS cached. On the contact path (which does not use IdentityNormalizationService and whose key already encodes its inputs — contactId + OrganizationIds via HashOptions, `MembershipResolverService.cs:1563-1564`, `:1602-1616`), the notes record why no fault-derived entry can be served for a different input, and a test pins that a resolve with an empty organization list does not satisfy a later resolve with the real organization ids.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">9. ACCESS SNAPSHOTS: a faulted inner snapshot (exception, 5xx, 429, timeout, OBO failure, user-lookup fault, probe transport fault or non-403/404 status, team/role sub-read fault) and a DEGRADED snapshot (RetrievePrincipalAccess unanswered → read-probe fallback) are NOT cached by CachedAccessDataSource on either GetUserAccessAsync or GetRecordAccessAsync, and DataverseAccessDataSource sets the fault indicator in each of those cases (exercised through the production classifier, not a stub snapshot). A legitimate None (RPA answered with no rights; probe 403/404; user lookup succeeded and found no systemuser) IS cached for 60 s. The faulted request itself still receives exactly the rights it receives today.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">10. AccessSnapshot's fault indicator defaults to not-faulted; every existing construction site compiles unchanged; the grep of other IAccessDataSource/AccessSnapshot consumers is recorded in the notes with "no behaviour change" per consumer.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">11. DEAD KEYS: CacheRolesAsync, CacheTeamsAsync, RolesTtl and TeamsTtl are deleted; the patterns `auth:roles` and `auth:teams` have zero hits under src/ and docs/architecture/ (covers both the `sdap:auth:roles` form in uac-access-control.md:61-62 and the `spaarke:tenant:{tenantId}:auth:roles` form in caching-architecture.md:65; the SpaarkeCore.cs:103-105 comment updated). Historical ADR text (docs/adr/, .claude/adr/) is out of this grep.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">12. TTLs: identity, membership and impersonated-root-set TTL constants equal the owner-approved values (proposed 2 min each); every comment/doc listed in step 7 states the new values; caching-architecture.md:335 is true of the code.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">13. INVALIDATION, teams/BU: after RegistrationDataverseService adds or removes a user from a team (and after the BU bind for an existing systemuser id) against this BFF's own environment, that user's identity entry, membership entries and impersonated root-set entries are gone — the next resolve re-reads (test asserts cache state, not timing). The same eviction happens when the call runs with NO HttpContext (the DemoExpirationService path) and the entries were written under a real tenant id. A write whose targetDataverseUrl names another environment evicts nothing and does not fail.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">14. INVALIDATION, owner (authorization case): after ProvisionProjectEndpoint re-owns a project to the Secure team, a BU colleague whose cached membership contained that project via ownership no longer receives it from the cache; the same holds for the colleague's cached impersonated root set for sprk_project, and for any cached access snapshot on that record (all users); the same for UnsecureProjectEndpoint's re-own. Asserted against cache state. A failed eviction does not fail the re-own (the endpoint's existing outcome is unchanged; the failure is logged).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">15. The invalidation in 13-14 is active in DEPLOYED configuration: either it does not depend on a kill-switch, or the switch is set in the three Bicep stacks (and their compiled .json) and in the dev App Service, evidenced in the notes — appsettings.template.json alone does not count. Symmetric real/Null-Object registration is preserved; a Null peer in a Redis-enabled environment logs once that invalidation is inert; the hook's XML doc names it as the call every owner-changing writer must make.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">16. DENY-VETO SUBJECT: the step-4 trace is recorded. If the fail-open is confirmed: on the systemuser plane, a FAULTED contact resolution denies every queried candidate (negative/authorization case), while a successfully-read systemuser with no linked contact composes exactly as before (no new denial) — both asserted. If not confirmed, the trace shows why, with file:line evidence.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">17. ADR-009: CachedAccessDataSource's keys follow the path recorded in the ADR tensions block (default C: ITenantCache with tenant + subject discrimination); the decision and the reviewer's approval are cited in the PR description. docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md:64 and :98-99 match the code.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">18. The final residual-staleness table (per cache: TTL, what invalidates it, outside-BFF bound, direction) is in docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md and the task notes, with the owner's sign-off (date + decision) recorded.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">19. PERTURBATIONS, each reverted before completion: re-enabling the cache write for a faulted grant set reddens 1; mapping a 429 to "answer" inside the grant-query classifier reddens 1 (proves the test reaches the classifier, not just the cache gate); removing the cancellation guard reddens 2 or 3; caching a faulted identity reddens 6; caching a faulted or degraded snapshot reddens 9; removing the re-own invalidation call, or only its impersonated-root-set part, reddens 14; changing the eviction pattern's resource segment by one character reddens 23.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">20. Build green; full BFF unit suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported (fresh master vs branch, short path, Compress-Archive, equal file counts) and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">21. MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev, after deploy; existing non-admin test users in their current BU; RECORD changes only): (a) in MDA, reassign a team-owned test matter away from the test user's team; poll the Teams/SPA matter list and record when it disappears — it must be within the signed-off outside-BFF bound. (b) Provision a secure test project from the wizard (or unsecure → re-secure the existing dev secure test project); a BU colleague who could see it before must, with a WARM cache (the colleague loads the project list immediately before provisioning), no longer see it on the first request after provisioning returns (invalidation), not after the TTL. Also check the colleague's direct record read of that project is denied on the first request. (c) Record the state of 036's `ExternalAccess:ImpersonatedRootSets:Enabled` flag during the gate; if 036 has merged, run (b) with the flag ON as well. Record users (by role), record ids, timestamps and responses in the notes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">22. Test scope: cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in the task notes; no near-identical happy-path variants.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">23. EVICTION REACHES THE REAL KEY: for every eviction pattern or key this task adds (identity, membership per-user and per-entity, impersonated root set, access snapshot), a test builds the key through the production writer path (production TenantCache over an in-memory IDistributedCache, with the configured InstanceName applied the way StackExchangeRedisCache applies it) and asserts the eviction pattern matches it — and does NOT match a neighbouring key (another user; another entity type; another record). An eviction that silently matches nothing cannot pass.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">24. PRE-FIX ENTRIES: IdentityNormalizationService.CacheVersion and MembershipResolverService.CacheVersion are bumped; a v-old entry seeded in the cache is not served after the change (one test per cache).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Defect C12 — `notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md` § "[MEDIUM] C12" (and UC1/UC2/UC6 caveats); owner decisions `notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md` rounds 1-2 (binding). Corrections carried from session 26: no access cache has a 15-min TTL (10 + 5 stacking); the roles/teams keys are write-only; ImpersonatedRootSetSource and MembershipEndpoints do not cache faults.

Adjacent but NOT this task (do not duplicate): #998 / task 109 (junction-fault deny veto — consumed here); C5 revocation task (CIAM-tid invalidation miss, grant-cache keying, org-grant revoke fan-out); task 131 (DirectAccessLevel in the grant cache); #1001 / task 112 (stale snapshot in the duplicate collapse on the WRITE path); #1010 (Manage Access modal renders a failed read as "no grants" — a client-side sibling of the same fault-looks-like-absence shape).

Coordination: the C10 re-own work (144 named owner team, 146 server child writers, 148 backfill and secure/unsecure transitions, 149 sharee access) adds or moves owner-changing writers — each must call the invalidation hook this task introduces. 141 (C7 identity) and 152 (notifications targeting) edit IdentityNormalizationService; 036 (C9) edits MembershipResolverService and AccessibleRecordSetService and turns ImpersonatedRootSetSource into the systemuser-plane source; 137 (C5) edits the grant-cache write/invalidation path. Sequence and rebase rather than merging blind; none of these is parallel-safe with this task.

Main-session follow-up (sub-agents cannot write .claude/): correct `.claude/constraints/auth.md:58` and `:98` to match ADR-003 A1.

GitHub: none exists (searched 2026-09-30); a new issue is proposed in this task's summary.</notes>

  <execution>
    <skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill>
